{
    "FormatVersion": 3,
    "DisplayName": "Cirrus",
    "Notes": "Ice, eight kilometres up, a quarter of a cumulus' extinction and a wispy edge cut by the finer of the two shipped noise volumes. The only type in the library that names a volume of its own, and the proof that a type's edge travels with the type. Anisotropy 8.00 IS THE POINT OF THIS FILE. Cirrus is not a field of thin blobs, it is fibrous bands combed out downwind by the shear ice falls through - and until a type carried its own placement field no profile, however authored, could turn a round patch into a band. Scale 0.60 with it, and the pair is what makes a FIBRE rather than a sheet: at the layer's 12 km tile the across-wind cell is about 1.80 km and the along-wind one about 14.4 km. Scale 2.50 was tried first and measured away - it makes each band 7.5 km across, which fills the zenith with one flat sheet and answers a different complaint than the one this file is for. SCALE 0.35 WAS ALSO MEASURED AWAY, and the reason given at the time was a coupling this file did not know it had: since the coverage field moved onto the billowy Alligator pair, the volume a type names decides its PLACEMENT CELLS as well as its edge, and CloudNoise_FineWisp halves both. THAT COUPLING NO LONGER EXISTS AND THIS NOTE IS THE ONLY PLACE IT SURVIVES - said here rather than deleted, because the number below still carries its shape. Phase E5 moved placement onto the lump lattice: BakeCloudProceduralVolume does not read a noise volume at all, and CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE has exactly one call site in the whole shader tree, the erosion. So a volume decides this type's EDGE and nothing else, and 0.60 is a number chosen against an argument that has since been removed from under it. What would change the answer: re-measuring the finest cell and the median chord against the march at 0.35 now that the volume cannot halve the cell. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md section NV records the finding; re-authoring the scale was not that task's. At 0.35 with that volume the finest cell was 160 m, the median chord of cirrus along a view ray was 128 m against a march that searches at 234 m, and three quarters of those chords were thinner than one search step - 3.66x past Nyquist, the worst in the library, and visible as dither along every strand. 0.60 puts the finest cell at 275 m, which is what Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudType now asserts against the schedule. DETAIL FACTOR 0.625, DOWN FROM 2.50, AND IT IS A RE-BASE RATHER THAN A RE-ART-DIRECTION. The depth of the cut is the layer's Detail Strength TIMES this number, and the 2.50 was authored when that strength was 0.10 - an effective cut of 0.25. Task DS raised the layer to 0.40 because below it the cut is finer than the march can resolve, and at 0.40 the old 2.50 gives an effective cut of 1.00: measured against a cloudless frame at the same camera, this type's contribution to the picture falls from 33 per cent of its un-eroded self to 4.3 per cent. A cirrus is thin - a DENSITY factor of 0.35 and an extinction of 0.25 - so a deep cut does not shred it, it deletes it. 0.625 restores the effective 0.25 the file was authored at, exactly - AND THE WORD EXACTLY BELONGS TO THE DEPTH ALONE. Detail Tile Size is a property of the LAYER and task DS also took it from 4 km to 1 km for all nine types, so this file now meets an erosion four times finer than anything it was authored against. Measured: the same amount of cirrus is in the sky to within 0.4 per cent, and 21.7 per cent of the frame's pixels move by at most 10 of 255 - the material is redistributed into shorter fibres, not removed. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md section DS carries the frames. What makes this type wispy is its DetailCharacter of 0 and the fine volume it names, not the depth of the cut. AND THE TOP OF THIS TYPE'S RANGE IS GONE ON PURPOSE: at 2.50 the layer's 0.40 drove it to the clamp, the deepest cut the maths allows, and it now tops out at 0.625 however far the layer's slider is pushed. That range rendered 4.3 per cent of the type - it did not make a more ragged cirrus, it made no cirrus. An artist who wants one needs a body that can carry a deeper cut: raise DensityFactor from 0.35 first and this factor after, with frames of its own. Detail Factor's slider runs to 8, so the range is there once the body can hold it. AND IT WAS RE-BASED A SECOND TIME, 0.625 -> 0.3846154, FOR THE SAME REASON AND BY THE SAME RULE - see Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md section SIL2. Task SIL2 raised the shape of the lump a body is built from (Assets::kCloudLumpVerticalOverHorizontal, 0.45 -> 0.75) so that a cloud reads as a body with volume instead of a plate; a taller lump is optically thicker per metre, so the same cut moves the visible surface less far, and the layer's Detail Strength had to go 0.40 -> 0.65 to keep clearing the chord the march can resolve. That multiplies every type's cut by 1.625, which is exactly what deletes this one. 0.65 x 0.3846154 = 0.250, the same effective depth this file has been authored at since before task DS: the cut has not moved at all, and Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudType asserts that identity on this asset rather than on the arithmetic in this note.",
    "NoiseVolume": "Clouds/CloudNoise_FineWisp.dcnv",
